    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ee7c69cc_8a2f78e7",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5036
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-23T17:04:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So trying to learn more about what exactly prevent_unsafe_narrowing is checking, I stumbled upon this doc, https://docs.google.com/document/d/1CTbQ-5cQjnjU8aCOtLiA7G6P0i5C6HpSDNlSNq6nl5E/edit. I\u0027m trying to get a better understanding about how strict of a philosophical goal it is to not just use static_cast to hide compiler warnings and don\u0027t do things like casting uint32_t\u0027s to ints.\n\nFor example, in this change in the SkMathPriv it is casting the uint32_t to an int and the function returns an int. Now reading that algorithm I know for a fact that the return will fit in an int so the cast is safe. But reading that doc it seems to suggest that instead we should be changing that whole function to return a uint32_t instead of an int so we don\u0027t need to do such a cast. Obviously this can have a very large ripple effect of need to update lots of callers.\n\nHow are you all handing/thinking about these sort of things in practice with these changes?",
      "revId": "db7eb4730a7c1bc17d61f5088a53dfc7b24c2294",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "335ba39f_b1c8336b",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 9153
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-23T17:54:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We will eventually turn this on, yes, but the problems have to be fixed before enabling the checks, and we\u0027re a long way from having fixed enough things to enable these checks in Skia.\n\n(For reference, over on the Chrome side, after many weeks of work I\u0027m getting close to having this fixed for base/ specifically for Windows builds.)",
      "parentUuid": "2b4f4a30_8391a480",
      "revId": "db7eb4730a7c1bc17d61f5088a53dfc7b24c2294",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4b6d0948_aaf57201",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 9153
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-23T17:54:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think the right answer is \"use the types that best express semantically what the functions are doing and that minimize casts, and if that requires significant API changes, so be it\".  For something like CLZ, I think the right answer is to be compatible with the C++20 STL function to do the same thing, which returns an int: https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/numeric/countl_zero .  That makes sense, because this is not \"a count of objects in a container\" or the like (which should be size_t), it\u0027s simply a \"normal countable number\".  For something like NextPow2, we have https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/numeric/bit_ceil as a reference, which returns the same type T as it accepts as input, which also makes some sense (and prompts the question of whether Skia\u0027s versions of these functions should be templated).",
      "parentUuid": "ee7c69cc_8a2f78e7",
      "revId": "db7eb4730a7c1bc17d61f5088a53dfc7b24c2294",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
